const sstAnchors={
 'The Rise of Nationalism in Europe':'Trace the shift from dynastic empires to nation-states through the French Revolution, liberalism, conservative reaction, unification and Balkan conflict.',
 'Nationalism in India':'Relate Rowlatt Satyagraha, Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience to different social groups, their aspirations and the limits of a single national unity.',
 'The Making of a Global World':'Compare pre-modern exchange, nineteenth-century trade and migration, interwar collapse and post-war institutions; globalisation has distinct historical phases.',
 'The Age of Industrialisation':'Factories did not instantly replace hand production: proto-industry, labour, Indian entrepreneurs and market-making coexisted unevenly.',
 'Print Culture and the Modern World':'Cheaper reproduction created new reading publics and debate, while authorities also used censorship; print shaped reform and nationalism without acting alone.',
 'Resources and Development':'Resource classification matters only when connected to planning, unequal access, land degradation, soil types and conservation.',
 'Forest and Wildlife Resources':'Biodiversity loss results from habitat pressure and exploitation; conservation ranges from protected areas to community management.',
 'Water Resources':'Scarcity reflects availability, demand, pollution and unequal access; large dams bring benefits and social/ecological conflicts, while harvesting works locally.',
 'Agriculture':'Connect seasons, crops, farming systems, technology, markets and institutional support instead of memorising crop lists.',
 'Minerals and Energy Resources':'Mineral occurrence, distribution and extraction reflect geology; conventional and renewable energy choices carry cost and environmental trade-offs.',
 'Manufacturing Industries':'Location depends on raw material, labour, capital, power, transport and market; industry creates linkages but also pollution needing control.',
 'Lifelines of National Economy':'Transport, communication, trade and tourism connect regions and markets; suitability differs by terrain, distance, cargo and cost.',
 'Power Sharing':'Belgium’s accommodation and Sri Lanka’s majoritarianism show why sharing power is both prudent for stability and morally democratic.',
 'Federalism':'Constitutional division of power, independent revenue/authority and courts define federalism; language policy and decentralisation explain Indian practice.',
 'Gender, Religion and Caste':'Social differences become political in different ways; reform and representation must be distinguished from communalism or caste exclusivism.',
 'Political Parties':'Parties recruit leaders, present policies, form governments and organise opposition, but face internal democracy, dynastic succession, money and limited-choice challenges.',
 'Outcomes of Democracy':'Evaluate democracy across accountability, responsiveness, economic performance, inequality, accommodation and dignity rather than expecting every outcome automatically.',
 'Development':'People value income alongside security, equality, freedom and public facilities; averages enable comparison but can hide distribution and sustainability.',
 'Sectors of the Indian Economy':'Classify activity by production, employment conditions and ownership; rising output does not guarantee adequate or secure jobs.',
 'Money and Credit':'Money removes the need for double coincidence, while credit outcomes depend on terms; formal institutions and self-help groups can reduce exploitative borrowing.',
 'Globalisation and the Indian Economy':'MNC investment, trade and production networks integrate markets; liberalisation creates opportunities and uneven pressure on workers and smaller producers.',
 'Consumer Rights':'Information asymmetry and unsafe or unfair practices justify rights, standards and redressal, but consumer responsibility and access to enforcement matter.'
};
